-- title:
--   Theorem 3.1 — $\mathcal S_\star$ and $\mathcal S'_\star$ are analytic, and Borel if $\Gamma$ is Borel
-- statement:
--   Consider the Borel-space MDP with analytic constraint graph $\Gamma$ and Borel transition kernel $q$, and let $\mathcal P(\Omega)$ carry the topology of weak convergence. For $\mathcal S_\star\in\{\mathcal S,\mathcal S_m,\mathcal S_s\}$ (strategic measures of all, Markov, or stationary policies), the sets $\mathcal S_\star$ and $\mathcal S'_\star$ (their nonrandomized subsets) are analytic subsets of $\mathcal P(\Omega)$. If $\Gamma$ is Borel, then $\mathcal S_\star$ and $\mathcal S'_\star$ are Borel.
--
--   This measurability is what allows the selection theorems of descriptive set theory to be applied to optimization problems over strategic measures.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: Borel subsets of $\mathcal P(\Omega)$ for the weak topology → measurable sets for Mathlib's $\sigma$-algebra on `ProbabilityMeasure`, generated by $p\mapsto p(B)$; reason: that $\sigma$-algebra is contained in the Borel one (equal for Borel spaces, Bertsekas–Shreve Prop. 7.25), so the conclusion is at least as strong
--   - encoding: Markov and stationary policies as kernels depending only on $x_n$ → kernels factoring through universally measurable kernels on $\mathbb A$ given $\mathbb X$; reason: the two descriptions give the same policies (restriction along a Borel map preserves universal measurability)
--   - encoding: analytic sets as the empty set or images of Borel subsets of Polish spaces under Borel maps (p. 5) → Mathlib's `AnalyticSet` (empty or a continuous image of the Baire space); reason: the two agree for subsets of a Borel space (Bertsekas–Shreve Prop. 7.41)
--   - encoding: $\Gamma$ Borel in $\mathbb X\times\mathbb A$ → `MeasurableSet M.graph` in the product $\sigma$-algebra; reason: Borel spaces are second countable, so the product $\sigma$-algebra is the Borel one
--   - encoding: $\rho_{p_0}[\pi]$ as the restriction of $\mathbb P^\pi_{p_0}$ to $\mathcal B(\Omega)$ → the predicate `IsStrategicMeasure M π p₀ P` ($x_0\sim p_0$, $a_n$ given $h_n$ follows $\mu_n$, $x_{n+1}$ given $h_n'$ follows $q$); reason: the predicate characterizes exactly that measure (compare Lemma 5.1), which exists and is unique by the milestone `strategicMeasure_existsUnique`
--   - generalization: the model includes a lower semianalytic one-stage cost $c$ → the model has no cost; reason: the statement does not involve $c$, so every model of the paper is an instance
-- source:
--   Yu, arXiv:2206.06492v3, Theorem 3.1, p. 7

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BertsekasShreve_AnalyticSelection_BorelSpace
import Definitions.Def_YuStrategic_Optimality_StrategicSets

namespace YuStrategic.Optimality

open MeasureTheory BertsekasShreve.AnalyticSelection

/-- **Theorem 3.1** (Yu, arXiv:2206.06492v3, p. 7). For `S⋆ ∈ {S, S_m, S_s}`, `S⋆` and `S′⋆` are
analytic subsets of `P(Ω)`; they are Borel if the graph `Γ` of the control constraint is Borel.

**Formalization Note.** `X`, `A` are Borel spaces with their Borel σ-algebras; `P(Ω)` carries
Mathlib's weak-convergence topology (for "analytic") and Mathlib's σ-algebra on
`ProbabilityMeasure`, generated by `p ↦ p(B)` (for "Borel"). The latter is contained in the Borel
σ-algebra of the weak topology (and equals it for Borel spaces), so the measurability conclusion
is at least as strong as the page's. "`Γ` Borel" is `MeasurableSet M.graph` in the product
σ-algebra of `X × A`, which is its Borel σ-algebra. -/
theorem theorem_3_1 {X A : Type} [TopologicalSpace X] [MeasurableSpace X] [BorelSpace X]
    [IsBorelSpace X] [TopologicalSpace A] [MeasurableSpace A] [BorelSpace A] [IsBorelSpace A]
    (M : Model X A) (κ : PolicyClass) :
    AnalyticSet (stratSet M κ) ∧ AnalyticSet (stratSet' M κ) ∧
      (MeasurableSet M.graph → MeasurableSet (stratSet M κ) ∧ MeasurableSet (stratSet' M κ)) := by sorry

end YuStrategic.Optimality
